                  "vocabulary": [
                    {
                      "term": "nominalisation",
                      "definition": "The grammatical process of turning verbs, adjectives, or clauses into abstract nouns.",
                      "example": "Replacing \"the climate is becoming warmer\" with \"global climatic warming\" is a prime example of nominalisation."
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "syntactic density",
                      "definition": "The concentration of information packed into complex noun phrases rather than distributed across multiple clauses.",
                      "example": "Academic abstracts achieve high syntactic density through embedded prepositional qualifiers."
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "depersonalisation",
                      "definition": "Removing explicit human agents to frame processes as objective institutional phenomena.",
                      "example": "Nominalisation aids depersonalisation by shifting focus from who decided to the decision itself."
                    }
                  ],
                  "grammar": {
                    "pattern": "Verb/Clause → Abstract Head Noun + Prepositional Qualifier + Post-modifying Clause",
                    "rule": "Nominal expressions package complex events into thematic concepts that can function as grammatical subjects or objects, enabling seamless progression from given to new information.",
                    "example": "\"Because the central bank raised rates unexpectedly, borrowing slowed\" → \"The central bank's unexpected rate hike precipitated a sharp deceleration in borrowing.\""
                  },
                  "explanation": "Nominalisation is the primary syntactic engine of academic, legal, and executive writing. By converting verbal actions into noun phrases (\"proliferate\" → \"proliferation\", \"deteriorate\" → \"deterioration\"), writers achieve greater concision, higher lexical density, and an objective, analytical tone. Rather than narrating sequences of actions (\"when companies merge, their cultures often clash\"), nominalisation treats complex occurrences as established conceptual phenomena (\"corporate mergers frequently induce organizational friction\"). However, skilled C1 writers balance nominal density against clarity to avoid bureaucratic obscurity.",
